Relevant columns and how they will be used  
• `event_date`: cohort filter (20180801‑20180815) and retention window (cohort + 8‑14 days).  
• `event_timestamp`: precise time‑difference calculations if needed.  
• `event_name`: identify quick‑play event types and the “session_start” events that define engagement / retention.  
• `user_pseudo_id`: unique user key for cohorting and retention calculations.  
• `event_params`: if a quick‑play type is stored in a param rather than in `event_name`.  
• `user_first_touch_timestamp`: secondary way to spot first use (not strictly needed because we will use the first `session_start`).  

Typical conditions  
• `_TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20180801' AND '20180815'` – cohort build period.  
• `_TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN cohort_date_plus_08 AND cohort_date_plus_14` – second‑week retention window.  
• `event_name = 'session_start'` – engagement / retention flag.  
• `LOWER(event_name) LIKE '%quickplay%'` – find quick‑play event types.  

Below are ten exploratory → incremental → final queries (all limited to 20 rows).  
Replace the project, dataset and table names only if you copied the data elsewhere.

```sql
-- 1) Description: list distinct event names that contain the word “quickplay”
